@h1 Conditionals h1@

A conditional shows content only when the data agrees. @@@code (if @path is
value) … (end) code@@@ compares; @@@code (if @path) … (end) code@@@ tests
truthiness. Use them for status banners, draft asides, and gated variants.

@aside(class note) 💡 Statements are whitespace-delimited: @@@code (if …) code@@@, @@@code (each …) code@@@, and @@@code (end) code@@@ are recognised only when the @code ( code@ follows whitespace or the start of a line. Keep a space or newline between adjacent statements — see the last section. aside@

@h2 Comparison h2@

@@@pre
#book: { status - draft }

(if @book.status is draft) DRAFT WATERMARK (end)
pre@@@

That renders the watermark (verified). The comparison keyword is @strong is
strong@ or @strong equals strong@ — they are @strong exact synonyms strong@
(fixtures 12-conditionals/is-comparison, equals-comparison). There is @strong no
strong@ other operator: no @code == code@, @code != code@, @code < code@,
@code > code@, and no @code not code@, @code and code@, @code or code@, or
@code contains code@.

@h2 Equality coerces to strings h2@

The right-hand side is a bareword or number, and equality stringifies both
sides. So a number compares equal to its digits:

@@@pre
#meter: { value - 99 }

(if @meter.value is 99) ON TARGET (end)
pre@@@

That is @strong true strong@ — the number @code 99 code@ stringifies to
@code 99 code@ (verified; fixture compare-against-number). A record or collection
stringifies to the empty string, so a container never equals a literal
(compare-against-string).

@h2 Existence and truthiness h2@

Drop the operator to test whether a value is present and truthy:

@@@pre
#book: { flag - true }

(if @book.flag) TRUTHY (end)
pre@@@

(fixture truthy-bare-reference.) The @strong falsy set strong@ is: missing or
unresolved, the empty string, the string @code false code@, the number
@code 0 code@, boolean @code false code@, null, an empty record @@@code {} code@@@,
and an empty collection @code [] code@. Everything else is truthy.

@h2 Else, empties, and nesting h2@

An @@@code (else) code@@@ branch is optional:

@@@pre
#book: { status - draft }

(if @book.status equals final) FINAL (else) NOT FINAL (end)
pre@@@

That renders @code NOT FINAL code@ (verified; fixture if-else-end). Empty then
or else branches are legal no-ops (empty-then-body, empty-else-body), and ifs
nest with their own @@@code (end) code@@@:

@@@pre
(if @lamp.lit)
  (if @lamp.oil is full) BRIGHT (end)
(end)
pre@@@

(nested-ifs; if-end-basic for the plain then-only form.)

@h2 The glued-statement gotcha h2@

Because statements need whitespace before the @code ( code@, a statement glued
to a preceding @code ) code@ is @strong not recognised strong@ and renders
literally:

@@@pre
(each @xs as x)(if @x is a)Y(end)(end)   ~ inner (if …) prints as text
(each @xs as x) (if @x is a) Y (end) (end)   ~ works
pre@@@

(verified.) Always separate adjacent statements with a space or newline.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Reaching for @code contains code@, @code < code@, @code > code@,
@code and code@, or @code or code@ — none exist. li@
@li Comparing a whole record to a literal — a container stringifies to empty and
never matches. li@
@li Gluing @@@code (if code@@@ to a preceding @code ) code@ — keep a space. li@
ul@

